  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"215\"><strong data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"155\">Phosphorus measurement in body fluids</strong> (e.g., pleural, peritoneal, or pericardial fluid) helps in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"217\" data-end=\"476\">\n<li data-start=\"217\" data-end=\"280\">\n<p data-start=\"219\" data-end=\"280\"><strong data-start=\"219\" data-end=\"252\">Evaluating causes of effusion</strong> (transudative vs exudative)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"343\">\n<p data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"343\"><strong data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"317\">Detecting local cell breakdown</strong>, infection, or malignancy</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"344\" data-end=\"405\">\n<p data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"405\"><strong data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"375\">Identifying urine leakage</strong> (especially in ascitic fluid)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"406\" data-end=\"476\">\n<p data-start=\"408\" data-end=\"476\">Supporting diagnosis in <strong data-start=\"432\" data-end=\"456\">tumor lysis syndrome</strong> or <strong data-start=\"460\" data-end=\"476\">pancreatitis</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"617\">Phosphorus in fluid is typically <strong data-start=\"511\" data-end=\"518\">low</strong> unless there is active cellular destruction or contamination with urine or intracellular contents.</p>\n<p data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"617\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Clinical Scenario</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation of Elevated Fluid Phosphorus</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malignancy-associated effusion</strong></td>\n<td>Tumor cell lysis may release intracellular phosphorus into the fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infectious effusions (e.g., TB, empyema)</strong></td>\n<td>Cell breakdown and inflammation raise phosphorus levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urinary leak (uroperitoneum)</strong></td>\n<td>Urine contains higher phosphate; elevated phosphorus suggests urinary ascites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute pancreatitis or ischemia</strong></td>\n<td>Pancreatic or bowel cell lysis can lead to elevated levels in peritoneal fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Peritoneal dialysis contamination</strong></td>\n<td>Dialysate high in phosphate may cause falsely elevated levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"617\">&nbsp;</p>",
